L132: # Cookbook — Approval-Gated Modules【0†¶】
L133: 
L134: > Type: User cookbook. Normative spec: 【74†PROTOCOL_SPEC §7】. Feature reference: 【45†features/approval-system.md】.
L135: 
L136: End-to-end recipe: a module that must be signed off before it runs, served by an `ApprovalHandler`. Phase A decides during the call; Phase B returns `pending`, and the caller retries later with `_approval_token`.
L137: ## When to use this pattern【20†¶】
L138: 
L139:   * The module does something that needs human or policy review: refunds, deletions, broadcast emails, infrastructure changes.
L140:   * You want the check enforced by the executor, where a caller cannot skip it.
L141:   * You need an audit trail of who approved what.
L142: ## When NOT to use this pattern【21†¶】
L143: 
L144:   * Checks that need no human: use input schema constraints or the module's `preflight()`.
L145:   * "May this caller use this module at all?" is an ACL question (【14†ACL Configuration Guide】). The ACL check runs before the approval gate.
L146:   * Rate limiting belongs in middleware.
L147: 
L148: * * *
L149: ## 1. Mark the module【22†¶】
L150: 
L151: `requires_approval` in the module's annotations makes the approval gate stop the call. The gate also fires when an ACL rule says `approval: required` or an `ExecutionPolicy` with `gate_destructive` matches a destructive module — see 【45†features/approval-system.md】.
