L481: ## 4. What happens when no handler is attached【25†¶】
L482: 
L483: With no `ApprovalHandler`, the gate is skipped with a warning and the module runs unapproved. To fail closed instead, attach an `ExecutionPolicy` with `strict` enabled — gated calls are then denied with `APPROVAL_DENIED` until a handler is attached:
L484: 
L485: [Input][Input]
L486: 
L487: Python TypeScript
L488: 
L489:     `from apcore import APCore, ExecutionPolicy
L490: 
L491:     client = APCore(policy=ExecutionPolicy(strict=True))
L492:     `
L493: ```typescript import { APCore, ExecutionPolicy } from 'apcore-js';
L494: 
L495: // ExecutionPolicy(rules, options)
L496: 
L497: const client = new APCore({ policy: new ExecutionPolicy(null, { strict: true }) }); ```
L498: 
L499: [Input]
L500: Rust
L501: 
L502:     `use apcore::{Config, Executor, ExecutionPolicy, Registry};
L503:     use std::sync::Arc;
L504: 
L505:     fn strict_executor() -> Executor {
L506:         let mut executor = Executor::new(Arc::new(Registry::new()), Arc::new(Config::default()));
L507:         executor.set_policy(Some(ExecutionPolicy::default().with_strict(true)));
L508:         executor // hand to APCore::with_options(None, Some(executor), None, None)
L509:     }
L510:     `
L511: 
L512: The approval handler is always attached in code; there is no `apcore.yaml` key for it.
L513: ## 5. Pitfalls【26†¶】
L514: Pitfall  | Symptom  | Fix
L515: --- | --- | ---
L516: No handler attached  | Warning at call time; the module runs without sign-off  | Attach a handler, or use `ExecutionPolicy(strict=True)` to fail closed
L517: Using `CallbackApprovalHandler` for Phase B  | Every resume fails with `APPROVAL_DENIED` ("Phase B not supported")  | Implement `check_approval()` in your own handler (section 3)
L518: Handler raises instead of returning `rejected`  | The caller gets the raised error (a non-apcore exception arrives as `MODULE_EXECUTE_ERROR`), not `APPROVAL_DENIED`  | Return an `ApprovalResult` with `status="rejected"` for a refusal; let only genuine failures raise
L519: Reusable tokens  | One sign-off approves many calls  | Consume the token in `check_approval()` (section 3); see 【76†security-considerations.md §2.3】
L520: Resuming re-runs the early pipeline  | ACL audit entries and approval events appear twice for one logical call  | Expected: a resume starts again at step 1 — context creation, call-chain guard, lookup, ACL, approval. Middleware `before` hooks run once, on the call that gets through
L521: Retrying `APPROVAL_TIMEOUT` in a tight loop  | Repeated prompts to the same reviewer  | Back off, or turn the timeout into a Phase B `pending`
L522: ## 6. Built-in handlers【27†¶】
L523: 
L524: Handler  | Use
L525: --- | ---
L526: `CallbackApprovalHandler(fn)`  | Phase A decisions from an async function
L527: `AutoApproveHandler`  | Tests and local development only — approves everything
L528: `AlwaysDenyHandler`  | Tests, or to block every gated module explicitly
